+@Zettelschrift "Native" Reference 2.0.0
=1

This is the reference for the "Native" (unhosted) version of Zettelschrift, a light markup language for personal knowledge management whose data structure is an incidence complex.

@Primitive elements and syntatic functions
=1/1

Zettelschrift is composed of nodes and freeform content as its primitive elements, and of containment and storage as its primitive relationships.

A node is anything that can be parsed as an independent addressable element. Containment is the action of a node addressing other nodes. Freeform content is anything that is not parseable as an independent addressable element. Storage is the action of attaching freeform content to a node.

From the point of view of Zettelschrift's formal data structure, all nodes exist at any time. From the point of view of a user writing and storing files with Zettelschrift, nodes can be virtual or real. A virtual node has no file that represents what is contained by it. A real node does.

A real node is written inside a file as such:

```
@The header's semantic address
=A header positional address
=Another header positional address

Freeform content and [[various other]] {{Zettelschrift}} [[body::elements]]. Zettelschrift requires an empty line between the header and the freeform content/other Zettelschrift elements.

```

You can choose to not parse a content by ```enclosing it with backticks. Line breaks are allowed, as shown previously```. You can escape a character by placing \ before it.

Zettelschrift uses syntatic functions to represent its primitive elements and the composite elements built from them.

@Addressing
=1/1/1

Addressing is the simplest way in which nodes are represented. A node has at least either one, and only one semantic address (plain title), and/or one or more positional addresses (nodeseqs). An exception is made for sequence nodes, which have their own special unique address and are described later in this reference. Granular addresses (nodesects), based upon either semantic or positional addresses, can also be used.

A node exists if, and only if, it has been addressed in some way. Addressing can be either direct (through writing a real node with a header), or indirectly through sequencing, reification, described later.

Different nodes cannot have the same semantic address. A node can have multiple positional addresses, but different nodes cannot have the same positional address.

@Semantic and positional addresses
=1/1/1/1
=4692/Mixed address/3

The semantic address of a node is a unique plain title assigned to it. In the header, it is written by placing an @, and then the actual plain title. The plain title cannot contain an @ as either its first or last character, nor + as its first character, nor / at any position, nor :: at any position, neither  [[ or ]] or {{ or }} at any position. A node can have one, and only one semantic address. Diferent nodes cannot have the same semantic address

A positional address of a node represents it through its full containment hierarchy, i.e. the full sequence of nodes that lead to it. It is written on the line just below the semantic address, by adding an =, and then the actual positional address. Each node in the sequence is separated through a /. It can be written either as an absolute numeric value (e.g. ```1/1/1/``` and ```1/1/2```, which are siblings of each other, and children of ```1/1```), the mere sequencing of semantic addresses (e.g. ```Zettelschrift "Native" Reference 2.0.0/Primitive elements and syntatic functions/Addressing/Semantic and positional addresses```), or a mixture of both notations (e.g. ```1/1/Addressing/Semantic and positional addresses```).

A node can have multiple positional addresses (i.e. it can have multiple parents), but different nodes cannot share the same exact positional address. Each additional positional address must be added as a newline in the header.

Positional addresses made from semantic addresses can be cyclic (i.e. ```=Mixed address/Addressing/1/Mixed address``` is perfectly valid). However, tautologies (i.e. ```=A/A```. or ```=A/A/A/B/B/B/B/A/A/A/A```) are not valid, and will be interpreted as redundant notation (i.e. the previous examples are equivalent to ```=A``` and ```A/B/A``` respectively).

There are no limits to a positional address' length. Addressing is creating: assigning a positional address to a node is already creating the containment relationships described by it.

@Sectoring
=1/1/2

Sectoring is a representation of a node and its freeform content/body elements form in a more reduced fashion than the regular header-body structure of a real node. It can conveniently add granularity by transforming what would otherwise be freeform content into a node itself. This paragraph can be transformed into a sector node by adding this after a line break:
=1=

Or this:
@A random sector@

Or, without line breaks, %by enclosing prose between %...%, hitting space, and adding this:% =2=. %Or this:% @Another random sector@.

A sector address can represent either a semantic address through @...@ notation, or a positional address through =...=. Sector addresses that are made from semantic addresses are independent from their neighbouring nodes. Sector addresses made from positional addresses, however, serve the function of a relative positional address, i.e. a positional sector address is the child of the last addressed (through a header) node before it.

The previous positional sector addresses' equivalent positional addresses  are, respectively, 1/1/2/1 and 1/1/2/2.  %The same freeform content can be assigned to multiple nodeseqs, like this:% =3= =4/10/1= @Yet another random sector@. Positional sector addresses accept the full syntax for regular positional address, but in this case, the ```=4/10/1=``` sector address is equivalent to the positional address 1/1/2/4/10/1, not to 4/10/1. 

+@Appending
=1/1/3

Appending is how nodes and sector nodes whose addresses already exist are represented. Nodes with identical semantic or positional addresses aren't allowed; however, Zettelschrift solves this not through an error, but by treating all instances of a repeated address as if they were a purposeful representation of a single node in a fragmentary fashion.

@Appending
=1/1/3

Any tool that interprets Zettelschrift will parse, interpret, store, and export the ```1/1/3``` node as:

```
@Appending
=1/1/3

Appending is how nodes whose addresses already exist are represented. Nodes with identical addresses aren't allowed; however, Zettelschrift solves this not through an error, but by treating all instances of a repeated address as if they were a purposeful representation of a single node in a fragmentary fashion.

Any tool that interprets Zettelschrift will parse, interpret, store, and export the ```1/1/3``` node as:

```...```

Sector nodes can also cause appending.

```

The same would apply for something like this: %Sector nodes can also cause appending.% @Appending@

@Appending priority
=1/1/3/1

Appending priority (i.e. which instance of a node should be treated as the one to receive appending) can be indicated manually by adding a + before any header marker. You may have already noticed at the start of this file. If this is not done, any Zettelschrift parser will use the date of creation to decide which instance of a node was the first one to exist. If this is not available, it will calculate this through the order inside of a document, and if this does not allow a decision, it will use the alphabetical order of the files containing Zettelschrift text.

@Sequencing
=1/1/4

Sequencing is how containment relationships, existing or new, can themselves be represented. A sequence is an (a priori) virtual node that represents the containment relationship between an origin node (i.e. the node in which the sequence notation was written) and a destiny node (i.e. the node contained by the origin node, whose containment is represented by the sequence node). Sequencing abstracts the functions of both regular and semantic hypérlinks/wikilinks into a single element, while expanding their possibilities.

A sequence node can take both positional and semantic addresses for their destiny node. A sequence node is written as [[...]], e.g. [[1/1]], [[Appending priority]], [[Addresses]], etc.

[[...]] means:

```
@Sequencing --sequence virtual node--> @...
```

Or, more explicitly:

```
@Sequencing --containment--> [[...]] sequence node --containment--> @...
```

@Composite destiny node
=1/1/4/1

Sequence nodes can take a composite destiny node, using :: as a separator for different addresses of the nodes to be composed, e.g. [[1/1::Sectoring::Reification]]. There is no limit for the number of nodes to be composed. 

[[10/2/3::B::C]] means:

```
@Composite destiny node --containment--> [[10/2/3::B::C]] virtual sequence node -containment--->
--> @10/2/3 --------------containment--> [[B::C]] virtual sequence node ---------containment--->
--> @B -------------------containment--> [[C]] virtual sequence node ------------containment---> @C
```

@Real sequence node and sequence address
=1/1/4/2

A sequence node is by default a virtual node. However, a sequence node can be transformed automatically (through any Zettelschrift tools) or manually into a real node.

A real sequence node must be written either like this:

```
&Real sequence node& [[Sequencing]]
@All addressing rules of regular nodes apply
=25/in the very
=24/same way

The header of a real sequence node includes its origin and its destiny node addresses, in the format ```&Origin node& [[Destiny node]]```. This is a special address called the sequence address. The inclusion of headers with a semantic address and positional addresses is optional, but allowed. Freeform content can be added to a real sequence node's body too. {{Zettelschrift}} [[body::elements]] can equally be added.
```

Or as a sector node: %This is a sector node that is also actually a sequence node.% =[[10/2]]=. By adding =[[ and ]]=, the dual status of the sector as a sector node is represented.

@Real independent sequence node
=1/1/4/2/1

A real independent sequence node is the representation of a sequence node appending: its content is appended to all sequence nodes that share the same destiny node.

It must be written either like this:

```
&[[Sequencing]]&
@All addressing rules of regular nodes apply
=26/in the very same way

Semantic addresses are allowed, but if this causes an appending to a node that isn't a sequence node, the sequence node will take priority, even if the non-sequence node was marked with + as the primary instance of the node.
```

Or like this: %This is a sector node that is also actually an independent sequence node.% @[[20/4]]@. By adding @[[ and ]]@, the dual status of the sector as an independent sector node is represented.

@Reification
=1/1/4/3

Reification is how sequencing can represent other instances of sequencing: sequence nodes can themselves be used as destiny nodes, or for making composite destiny nodes.

Reification is done simply by writing a sequencing inside another sequencing, e.g. [[demonstrates::[[Zettelschrift::is::recursive]]]], which means:

```
@Reification --> [[demonstrates::[[Zettelschrift::is::recursive]]]] -->
--> @demonstrates --------------> &demonstrates& [[Zettelschrift::is::recursive]]

&demonstrates& [[Zettelschrift::is::recursive]] real sequence node --->
--> @Zettelschrift --------------> [[is::recursive]] ----------------->
--> @is -------------------------> [[recursive]] ---------------------> @recursive
 
```

@Composite destiny nodes and reification inside of a sequence address
=1/1/4/3/1

Composite destiny nodes and reification can be represented in real sequence nodes. 

The second step of a previous example,

```
@Composite destiny node --containment--> [[10/2/3::B::C]] virtual sequence node -containment--->
--> @10/2/3 --------------containment--> [[B::C]] virtual sequence node ---------containment--->
--> @B -------------------containment--> [[C]] virtual sequence node ------------containment---> @C
```

can be represented in a sequence address as:

```
&[[10/2/3::B::C]]& [[B::C]]
``` 

And the second step of another previous example,

```
@Reification --> [[demonstrates::[[Zettelschrift::is::recursive]]]] -->
--> @demonstrates --------------> &demonstrates& [[Zettelschrift::is::recursive]]

&demonstrates& [[Zettelschrift::is::recursive]] real sequence node --->
--> @Zettelschrift --------------> [[is::recursive]] ----------------->
--> @is -------------------------> [[recursive]] ---------------------> @recursive
 
```

can be represented in a sequence address as:

```
&[[demonstrates::[[Zettelschrift::is::recursive]]]]& [[Zettelschrift::is::recursive]]
```

@Positional sequence
=1/1/4/4

A positional sequence represents a composite destiny node made of all the nodes of a positional address. It is written as {{...}}, e.g. {{1/1/1}}.

{{1/1/1}} is the same as [[1::1/1::1/1/1]].

@Sequence list
=1/1/4/5

A sequence list is an alternative notation for sequencing. It allows writing complex, reified sequencing in a more natural and readable fashion. It abstracts the functions of ToCs/indexes.

```
@Zettelschrift's Syntax
=0

*[[Primitives]]
**[[Elements]]
***[[Nodes]]
***[[Freeform content]]
*[[Syntatic functions]]
**[[Addressing]]
**[[Sectoring]]
**[[Appending]]
**[[Sequencing]]
```

A sequence list such as:

```
@A
=100

*[[B]]
**[[C::D]]
**[[28]]
***[[E::[[F::G]]]]
*[[28]]
**[[29]]
```

contains the sequence nodes ```[[B::[[C::D]]]]```, ```[[B::28::[[E::[[F::G]]]]]]```, and ```[[28::29]]```.

@1/2
